WEBVTT

resistance-01-1
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.910
Resistance will never succeed.

resistance-01-2
00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:10.370
Here, ‘success’ means no longer creating
new centers and new margins.

resistance-01-3
00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.460
This is a proposition to investigate
throughout this series, not a conclusion.

resistance-01-4
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.932
But before discussing resistance, let us
start with something that comes before

resistance-01-5
00:00:19.932 --> 00:00:20.140
it.

resistance-01-6
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.530
Episode 1: Is Anxiety More Frightening
Than Danger?

resistance-01-7
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.270
At night, there is a wolf in the woods.

resistance-01-8
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.500
She is afraid. But she knows what she is
afraid of.

resistance-01-9
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.020
Run. Hide. Fight.

resistance-01-10
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:47.220
This is fear: it has an object. Knowing
the object means knowing what to do.

resistance-01-11
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.020
The same woods. This time, nothing is
there.

resistance-01-12
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.320
Yet she still feels uneasy. She cannot
say what she is afraid of.

resistance-01-13
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.212
This is anxiety: it has no object.
Without an object, you do not know which

resistance-01-14
00:01:03.212 --> 00:01:03.930
way to run.

resistance-01-15
00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:12.480
Pause for a moment: can you name what is
worrying you right now?

resistance-01-16
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.330
This distinction has a long history in
philosophy.

resistance-01-17
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:22.024
Object — Fear: a specific threat.
Anxiety: no clear object; philosophers

resistance-01-18
00:01:22.024 --> 00:01:23.980
call its object ‘nothingness.’

resistance-01-19
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.045
Response — Fear: two options, fight or
flight. Anxiety: many possibilities,

resistance-01-20
00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:29.660
including repetition, freezing, rushing
out, or even excitement.

resistance-01-21
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.405
Rumelili 2020, pp. 258–259: the
existentialist tradition (Kierkegaard,

resistance-01-22
00:01:31.405 --> 00:01:33.324
Heidegger, Tillich) distinguishes fear
from anxiety: fear concerns a specific

resistance-01-23
00:01:33.324 --> 00:01:34.520
object; anxiety has ‘nothingness’ as its
object.

resistance-01-24
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.629
Kinnvall &amp; Mitzen 2020, p. 241: fear
leads to ‘fight or flight’; anxiety lacks

resistance-01-25
00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:39.940
a clear object and may elicit many
emotions and behaviors.

resistance-01-26
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:47.410
The theologian Tillich distinguished fear
of death from anxiety about death.

resistance-01-27
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:51.450
If you fear a car crash, you can fasten
your seat belt.

resistance-01-28
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.630
But there is no seat belt for ‘one day, I
will no longer exist.’

resistance-01-29
00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.070
Tillich’s distinction, as recounted in
Rumelili 2020, p. 259.

resistance-01-30
00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:09.320
So people do something strange: they find
an object for their anxiety.

resistance-01-31
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.890
Because only fear can be confronted,
attacked, or endured.

resistance-01-32
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.861
Tillich’s view, as recounted in Rumelili
2020, p. 259; p. 267: unlike nothingness,

resistance-01-33
00:02:15.861 --> 00:02:16.990
objects can be managed and fought back
against.

resistance-01-34
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:22.690
Drawing a wolf feels better than facing
the dark.

resistance-01-35
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.470
States can do this too.

resistance-01-36
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:32.770
Two countries have argued for decades.
Arguing has become their daily routine.

resistance-01-37
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.650
When peace finally comes, they become
restless instead.

resistance-01-38
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:42.284
Conflict at least gave them a definite
object; peace releases the anxiety they

resistance-01-39
00:02:42.284 --> 00:02:43.440
had kept at bay.

resistance-01-40
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.143
Mitzen 2006, p. 341: even dangerous
routines provide ontological security;

resistance-01-41
00:02:45.143 --> 00:02:46.984
states may become attached to conflict.
Rumelili 2020, p. 268: conflict offers a

resistance-01-42
00:02:46.984 --> 00:02:48.710
definite object of fear; prospects of
peace may release suppressed anxiety.

resistance-01-43
00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:55.495
This does not mean peace is bad. It means
people and states seek more than

resistance-01-44
00:02:55.495 --> 00:02:56.320
survival.

resistance-01-45
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:04.600
He has retired. He has enough savings,
good health, and no danger to face.

resistance-01-46
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.504
On the first Monday, his alarm still
rings at six thirty. He picks up his

resistance-01-47
00:03:09.504 --> 00:03:11.250
briefcase, then remembers—

resistance-01-48
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.420
He does not have to go.

resistance-01-49
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.760
He is safe. Yet suddenly, he does not
know who he is now.

resistance-01-50
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.180
Ontological security

resistance-01-51
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.430
It asks not ‘Can I survive?’ but ‘Am I
still me?’

resistance-01-52
00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.976
Kinnvall &amp; Mitzen 2020, p. 240: following
Giddens, international relations scholars

resistance-01-53
00:03:31.976 --> 00:03:34.368
understand it as a need for cognitive
consistency and continuity in one’s life

resistance-01-54
00:03:34.368 --> 00:03:35.350
narrative—a security of ‘being.’

resistance-01-55
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:40.298
The things we do each day without
thinking shield us from what lies behind

resistance-01-56
00:03:40.298 --> 00:03:40.900
the wall.

resistance-01-57
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.231
Giddens 1991, p. 36: ‘chaos lurks’ behind
everyday routines. Cited in Mitzen 2006,

resistance-01-58
00:03:43.231 --> 00:03:43.430
p. 346.

resistance-01-59
00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:51.780
Count them: how many things do you do
each day without thinking?

resistance-01-60
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.340
So when does anxiety arise?

resistance-01-61
00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.517
Reading 1: anxiety is always there. The
self is always divided, even when we do

resistance-01-62
00:04:01.517 --> 00:04:03.680
not notice. (A Lacanian reading.)

resistance-01-63
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.114
Reading 2: people do not feel anxious all
the time as they go about their lives.

resistance-01-64
00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:12.050
Anxiety is rare and temporary. (A reading
following Laing and Giddens.)

resistance-01-65
00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.700
For a comparison of these readings, see
Krickel-Choi 2022, p. 15.

resistance-01-66
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.340
This series takes a third path.

resistance-01-67
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.940
She imagines herself a year from now:
giving a speech onstage in Japanese.

resistance-01-68
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:30.228
Her future self can only be imagined.
While imagining it, she feels

resistance-01-69
00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:33.070
secure—anxiety has not yet appeared.

resistance-01-70
00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:39.990
Until she signs up and begins trying to
make that self real.

resistance-01-71
00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:46.149
A gap opens between who she is now and
the self she imagines. Anxiety grows in

resistance-01-72
00:04:46.149 --> 00:04:46.860
that gap.

resistance-01-73
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.000
This series’ proposition

resistance-01-74
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.320
Anxiety is not always lurking there.

resistance-01-75
00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.400
It grows out of ‘wanting to become.’

resistance-01-76
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:04.760
And it has no object—so it is always
looking for one.

resistance-01-77
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.730
Those who hold on fear losing the self
they already are.

resistance-01-78
00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.210
Those who strive are eager to become a
self they are not yet.

resistance-01-79
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.950
Both anxieties are looking for an object.
Then they find each other.

resistance-01-80
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.920
The chair is still there.

resistance-01-81
00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.860
Next time, we continue asking:

resistance-01-82
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:34.510
Can security be ‘realized’? Or can it
only be ‘felt’?

resistance-01-83
00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.720
This is UjiBricks.
